# Scroll — One-Page Infographic Spec

**Deliverable:** a single portrait page (Letter/A4, 1× bleed, 300 dpi) that a stranger can read top-to-bottom in ~90 seconds and walk away able to say what Scroll is, why it is different, and what makes it tick.
**Voice:** plain, confident, a little dry-humored. No buzzwords. "Scientists of all ages."
**Anchor line:** *Publish and evolve your most intelligent ideas.*
**Version stamp:** Scroll v180.2.0 · scroll.surf

---

## Canvas & Grid

| Zone | Height | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Hero | 18% | Name, tagline, three-word definition |
| 2. The Stack | 14% | Particles → Parsers → Scroll |
| 3. Why it matters | 24% | The 6 game-changing benefits |
| 4. Tiny details | 26% | The dense "it hums" grid |
| 5. Numbers band | 8% | Proof strip |
| 6. Footer | 10% | Install, links, license, motto |

Palette: warm paper `#faf9f6`, ink `#29313d`, muted `#626975`, scroll blue `#315b8f`. One accent per zone, no gradients. Type: humanist sans for body, mono for all code. Every code sample must be real and copy-pasteable.

---

## Zone 1 — Hero (top 18%)

- **Eyebrow (mono, muted):** `scroll`
- **Headline (huge):** **Scroll is a language for scientists of all ages.**
- **Subhead:** Publish and evolve your most intelligent ideas.
- **Three-word definition, set as three stacked chips:**
  1. `Words become Particles`
  2. `Connected by Parsers`
  3. `To guide the world`
- **One-sentence explainer:** A text format, a static-site generator, and an extendible language in one — as readable by hand as by a machine.
- **Smallest real example beside the headline:**
  ```
  A link to Wikipedia
   https://wikipedia.org Wikipedia
  ```
  Caption: *That is the whole syntax. A line, then a more-indented line.*

---

## Zone 2 — The Stack (14%)

Three stacked boxes, bottom-to-top, with a one-line role each:

| Layer | What it is | Role |
|---|---|---|
| **Scroll** | a language made of parsers | publish docs, sites, data |
| **Parsers** | a language for making languages | define and inherit new parsers |
| **Particles** | the syntax (atoms on lines + indent) | split a document into pieces |

- Callout: **"Each line is almost its own file."** The off-side rule (a single extra space) scopes every line.
- Tiny footnote: *Master the indent trick and you master Scroll.*

---

## Zone 3 — Why it matters (24%)

Six benefit cards, 2×3. Each card = icon + 4-word title + one sentence + one proof.

1. **One file, many languages.**
   Add a Markdown, Textile, or BBCode block — or your own — with no escaping, because indentation scopes each block. *Proof: `indented-heredocs.scroll` composes three markup languages in one document.*
2. **A language, not a generator.**
   Define new parsers right inside your file; your symbolic tools grow with your thinking. *Proof: a working `recipeParser` in ~6 lines.*
3. **Timeless plain text.**
   Designed to be useful in 1800 and still useful in 2200 — it works on pen and paper and in git. *Proof: one article = one file, publicly tracked.*
4. **One source, every output.**
   Write once; emit HTML, TXT, CSV, TSV, JSON, and RSS. *Proof: a ScrollSet is a text file that is also a CSV and a schema.*
5. **Structured data without a database.**
   ScrollSets mix schema, docs, citations, and data in one file, then export clean tables. *Proof: PLDB — 100,000+ atoms, 1,000s of pages.*
6. **Quiet by default.**
   Output HTML ships no third-party JavaScript, no cookies, no trackers — only tiny inline enhancements. *Proof: view-source of scroll.surf.*

---

## Zone 4 — Tiny details that make it hum (26%)

A dense, scannable grid (mono labels). These are the details that separate Scroll from "another Markdown."

**How it compiles — one pass**
Scroll is a **single-pass language** (since v177, April 2025). The whole file is streamed through one pass: imports, macros, and parser definitions resolve as they are encountered, top to bottom. A macro therefore only affects lines *after* it.

**Syntax details**
- Atoms split on a single space; particles split on newline; **one extra space = subparticle**
- The **catch-all is the paragraph** — plain text just works
- **Aftertext:** markup comes *after* the text it affects, so prose stays clean
  ```
  The theory of relativity
   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relativity theory of relativity
  ```
- `//` comments print nothing; `***` dinkus breaks a section; `****` ends a post
- `?` question, `!` counterpoint, `[]` / `[x]` checklists, `>` quote, `#`/`##` headers
- `blink` exists as a joke (with a "please don't use this" note)

**Project details**
- `importOnly` — import a file but don't build it (perfect for `header.scroll` / `footer.scroll`)
- `moveToFooter` — pull an import to the bottom
- Circular imports are detected and reported
- Macros: `replace YEAR 2022`, plus `replaceJs SUM 1+1`
- Themes are just CSS: `roboto · gazette · dark · tufte · prestige`
- `buildTxt` / `buildHtml` / `buildCsv` / `buildTsv` / `buildJson` / `buildRss`
- RSS via a `feed.scroll`; sitemap via `sitemap.scroll`; custom 404 via `404.scroll`
- Open Graph auto-fills from the article's first image and first paragraph
- `tags` parser auto-builds category pages
- Git-over-RSS: `<link rel="source" type="application/git" …>` in every page head
- `assert` parsers make documents self-testing
- Hot reload is one alias: `nodemon -e scroll -x "scroll build"`

**ScrollSets (data in text)**
- Four atoms: **concepts** (rows), **measures** (columns/schema), **values**, **measurements**
- Rows start `id mars`; measures are `moonsParser` extending an abstract type
- Nested comments are stripped from the CSV/TSV export but kept in source
- `buildConcepts` + `buildMeasures` generate tables *about* your data

**Microlanguages already shipped**
`based64` (64×64 image as ASCII), `stamp` (60-line project templates), `counter`, `heatrix`, `sparkline`, `plot`, `dashboard`, `columns`, `forms`, `slideshow`, `qrcode`, `katex`, `maps`

**Parsers as a system**
- Inheritance + atom types + regex matching
- The **cue** (first atom) is usually the most important information and drives matching
- Like Lisp's best ideas, minus the parentheses

